/* ── Game 09 · the Immaculate Grid ──────────────────────────────────
   Nine squares. Each is the intersection of the criterion on its row and
   the criterion on its column, and the entire game is whether a fan can
   READ that intersection in half a second. So the interface never shuts
   up about it:

     · hover or select a square and both of its headers light up
     · the side panel writes the square out as a sentence a fan would
       actually say. "Name a player with a 1,000-faceoff season as a
       Leaf", because "TOR × FO1000" is a query, not a game
     · a wrong guess burns one of the nine but the square STAYS OPEN,
       which is how the real game plays; locking a square on a miss
       punished curiosity and that is why it felt dead
     · when the guesses run out, every open square reveals its most-
       picked answer, so the board always ends in knowledge, not a shrug

   Rules of eligibility are unchanged and still database-verified:
   a club square means he played a game for that club (ice time > 0);
   a stat square means one season; crossed, that season is with that club. */

